#{[17592186478392 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478392 #edb/ref 1069 "q-62813" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478392 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478392 #edb/ref 1071 "The price of crude oil, measured in dollars per barrel, increases at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\frac{2}{1+3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in dollars per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the start of a particular year.\n\nIf the price was $$25$ per barrel at the start of the year, which expression gives the price of crude oil $t$ months since the start of the year?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478392 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3cbd327e-e7fe-5c08-8abb-efce49ecca7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478392 #edb/ref 1074 "We're given the **rate of change** of the oil price $r(t).$ To find the **total price** after $t$ months (call it $P(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{2}{1+3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =25+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{2}{1+3x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478399 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478399 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90749" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478399 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478399 #edb/ref 1071 "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=25+3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478399 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "480dd21a-6ad7-5569-99ae-370c6e08eaac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478399 #edb/ref 1074 "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(25+3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(25)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(8-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n3(8-2t)=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=4,$ we figure out $N(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(4) & =25+3{\\int }_{0}^{4}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =25+3{[8x-{x}^{2}]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =25+3[(8\\cdot 4-{4}^{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =25+3\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =73\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $25<73$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $73$, and this will happen after $4$ hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478411 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478411 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478411 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478411 #edb/ref 1071 "The rate $r(t)$ at which the balance in a particular savings account grows is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=20{e}^{0.05t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in thousands of dollars per year, and $t>0$ is the time in years.\n\nTo the nearest thousand dollars, how much did the account's balance increase between years $t=2$ and $t=4?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478411 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6686da9c-8c33-5aec-9383-87dd4a554b8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478411 #edb/ref 1074 "We're given the **rate of change** of the account's balance $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in the balance between the times $t=2$ and $t=4,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}20{e}^{0.05t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={20\\,\\frac{{e}^{0.05t}}{0.05}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =400{e}^{0.05t}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =400({e}^{0.2}-{e}^{0.1}) \\\\  & ≈46\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, between years $t=2$ and $t=4,$ the account's balance increased by $$46\\,000.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478415 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478415 #edb/ref 1069 "q-349971" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478415 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478415 #edb/ref 1071 "The rate at which customers enter a store one afternoon is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=6{e}^{t}+3,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in customers per hour and $t>0$ is the time in hours since $1\\,\\text{p.m}.$\nCustomers are also leaving the store at a steady rate of $5$ customers per hour.\n\nIf there were $40$ customers in the store at $1\\,\\text{p.m},$ how many customers will be there at $4\\,\\text{p.m}?$\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, there will be \n{{field-1}}  customers in the store at $4\\,\\text{p.m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478415 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "70deaba5-9010-5ebe-8348-672c8fc97ab0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478415 #edb/ref 1074 "Customers are entering the store at a rate of $6{e}^{t}+3$ customers per hour and are leaving at a rate of $5$ customers per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of customers (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-5 \\\\  & =6{e}^{t}+3-5 \\\\  & =6{e}^{t}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of customers** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{e}^{t}-2)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the number of packages after $3$ hours ($4\\,\\text{p.m}$), we substitute $t=3$ into the above and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3) & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6{e}^{t}-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =40+(6{e}^{t}-2t){|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =40+[(6{e}^{3}-2(3))-(6{e}^{0}-2(0))] \\\\  & =40+6{e}^{3}-12 \\\\  & =6{e}^{3}+28 \\\\  & ≈149\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, there will be $149$ customers in the store at $4\\,\\text{p.m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478416 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478416 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90730" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478416 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478416 #edb/ref 1071 "People enter the ballpark at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function \n\n$$\n10t+5,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per minute,  and $t>0$ is the time in minutes since the ballpark gates were opened to the public.  \n\nHow many people entered the ballpark between $t=10$ minutes and $t=20$  minutes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478416 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "722ff44e-d1e5-509c-bcea-de9f4cf913cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478416 #edb/ref 1074 "We're given the **rate of change** of the number of people $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in the number of people who entered the ballpark between  $t=10$ minutes and $t=20$ minutes,  we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{10}^{20}10x+5\\,\\text{d}t & =5({x}^{2}+x){|}_{10}^{20} \\\\  & =5[({20}^{2}+20)-({10}^{2}+10)] \\\\  & =5\\cdot (420-110) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (310) \\\\  & =1\\,550\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between $t=10$ minute and $t=20$ minutes,  a total of $1\\,550$ people entered the ballpark." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478434 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478434 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140545" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478434 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478434 #edb/ref 1071 "The rate at which the number of visitors at a particular festival changes is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=4t+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in thousands of people per hour, and $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since the festival opened its gates.\n\nHow many thousands of people did the number of visitors increase by between hours $t=1$ and $t=3?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b00e0f7a-d41e-5bb8-bfda-7014077ce7a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478434 #edb/ref 1074 "We're given a **rate of change** in the number of visitors $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in the number of visitors between $t=1$ and $t=3$ hours, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(4t+2)\\,\\text{d}t & ={(2{t}^{2}+2t)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(2(3)^{2}+2(3))-(2(1)^{2}+2(1)) \\\\  & =18+6-2-2 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between hours $t=1$ and $t=3,$ the number of visitors increased by $20$ thousand people." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478446 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478446 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478446 #edb/ref 1069 "q-349995" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478446 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478446 #edb/ref 1071 "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=25+3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478446 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c581124e-b7b1-5b7e-bfb5-1ff3fee7303f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478446 #edb/ref 1074 "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(25+3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(25)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(8-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n3(8-2t)=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=4,$ we figure out $N(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(4) & =25+3{\\int }_{0}^{4}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =25+3{[8x-{x}^{2}]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =25+3[(8\\cdot 4-{4}^{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =25+3\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =73\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $25<73$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $73$, and this will happen after $4$ hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478450 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478450 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346832" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478450 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478450 #edb/ref 1071 "Environmental scientists are studying the population of fish in a lake.\nThey observe that the fish population increases due to reproduction at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=40{t}^{2}+6t,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in fish per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the study began.\nAt the same time, $10$ fish are lost each month due to predation.\n\nIf there were initially $500$ fish in the lake, find the expression that gives $P(t),$ the number of fish in the lake $t$ months after the beginning of the study.\n\n$P(t)=\\frac{40}{3}{t}^{3}\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478450 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d1f89285-0002-5f93-8ef8-535c491c51d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478450 #edb/ref 1074 "The population of the lake is increasing at a rate of $r(t)$ fish per month and is decreasing at a rate of $10$ fish per month. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the number of fish is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-10 \\\\  & =40{t}^{2}+6t-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number $P(t)$ of fish after $t$ months, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =500+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(40{x}^{2}+6x-10)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =500+{\\int }_{0}^{t}40{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{t}6x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}10\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =500+{40\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{t}+{6\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{t}-10x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =500+\\frac{40}{3}{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-10t \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-10t+500\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478466 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478466 #edb/ref 1069 "q-349763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478466 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478466 #edb/ref 1071 "A factory is tracking the rate at which plastic pellets are produced. The rate $r(t)$ at which pellets are produced is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=0.4\\sqrt{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in kilograms per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes.\n\nBetween $t=1$ and $t=10$ minutes, to one decimal place,\n{{field-1}} kilograms were produced." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478466 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fdea6428-2f62-5007-88f6-e327c0da6446" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478466 #edb/ref 1074 "We're given the **rate of change** in the amount of plastic pellets produced, $r(t).$ To find the amount of plastic pellets produced between the times $t=1$ and $t=10$ minutes, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{10}0.4\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={0.4\\cdot \\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}|}_{1}^{10} \\\\  & ={\\frac{0.8}{3}{t}^{3/2}\\,|}_{1}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.8}{3}(10)^{3/2}-\\frac{0.8}{3}(1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{10}}{3}-\\frac{4}{15} \\\\  & ≈8.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place. \n\nTherefore, between $t=1$ and $t=10$ minutes, $8.2$ kilograms were produced." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
